in circulation pump gate is biased high through R5 whenever the T1 pre-driver is off, so the 24 V pump can run unintentionally at power-up, reset or during an MCU fault. That is a real safety and operational risk for the nutrient loop.\",\n      \"confidence\": 0.45\n    },\n    {\n      \"category\": \"open_connection\",\n      \"components\": [\"U1\"],\n      \"pins\": [\"U1.2\"],\n      \"nets\": [\"unconnected-(U1-EN-Pad2)\"],\n      \"observed\": \"The TPS62933 buck regulator U1 has its EN pin (pin 2) marked as no-connect and not attached to any net, so the enable input is left floating.\",\n      \"expected\": \"EN should be tied to VIN, through a divider if needed, or driven by the MCU. A floating enable on a 24 V-input buck is not a sound practice and makes start-up behaviour undefined.\",\n      \"evidence\": [\n        {\"source\": \"netlist\", \"quote\": \"(net (code \\\"136\\\") (name \\\"unconnected-(U1-EN-Pad2)\\\") (class \\\"Default\\\") (node (ref \\\"U1\\\") (pin \\\"2\\\") (pinfunction \\\"EN\\\") (pintype \\\"input+no_connect\\\")))\"},\n        {\"source\": \"spec\", \"quote\": \"U1 \u2014 TPS62933 (TI) | 24 V \u2192 5 V synchronous buck regulator, 3 A\"}\n      ],\n      \"impact\": \"The 5 V rail that powers the ESP32 and the LDO may fail to start, or may start unreliably, depending on the part's internal enable default.\",\n      \"confidence\": 0.25\n    }\n  ],\n  \"summary\": \"The most likely deviation is R5 on the main pump gate (G_PUMP) pulled up to +3V3 instead of pulled down to GND as on the ATO stage, which would leave the 24 V pump enabled by default. A floating EN pin on the TPS62933 buck is a lower-confidence second candidate.\"\n}",
